<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Steve Biko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1946">1946</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bantu Stephen Biko</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Steve Biko was a South African anti-apartheid activist and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness Movement</a>, which urged black South Africans to free themselves from psychological dependence on whites. Banned from 1973, he was arrested in August 1977 and died in police custody in September after severe beatings. His death caused international outrage and contributed to the mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> arms embargo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/steve-biko.jpg" alt="Steve Biko" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cape Argus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Steve_Biko_on_Flyer_for_Steve_Biko_Memorial_at_the_Carver_Cultural_Center.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within the country, the Soweto uprising of 1976, the Black Consciousness movement of <b>Steve Biko</b>, who died in police custody in 1977, and the United Democratic Front, founded in 1983, created new forms of mass resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 1960s the Black Consciousness Movement of <b>Steve Biko</b> urged Black people, a term that for Biko included Africans, Indians and coloureds, to free themselves from psychological dependence on white liberals and build their own organisations.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Its title is a Zulu phrase that may be translated &quot;We have not seen him&quot;, and the song alludes to Mandela's absence from society and names other activists against apartheid, among them <b>Steve Biko</b>, Victoria Mxenge and Neil Aggett.</blockquote><a href="/songs/asimbonanga">Asimbonanga</a></li><li><blockquote>A recording of the song as sung at the funeral of <b>Steve Biko</b> can be heard at the end of the album version of Peter Gabriel's &quot;Biko&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/senzeni-na">Senzeni Na?</a></li></ul></section>
